{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788297485-bfxaQYWPOujG7rlzKuQy","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Analysis of merges:\n\n**96f074065** (merge #1898 branch): The \"combined diff\" (git show on a merge shows files differing from all parents) shows the #1898 run dir + 2 product files. Hmm wait — that's suspicious. `git show` on a merge shows the combined diff, which lists files whose result differs from BOTH parents. For a clean merge where branch-side didn't touch those files, the result equals parent1's version... no wait. The #1898 branch's files: parent1 (a7206cd3d, branch side) did NOT have the #1898 changes; parent2 (438ed4f60) HAS them; result has them. So the result differs from parent1 but matches parent2 → NOT a combined-diff entry. For a file to appear in combined diff, it must differ from BOTH parents — e.g., a conflict resolution taking parts of each, or a manual edit.\n\nHmm, but `git show --stat` on merges... Actually for merge commits, `git show` displays the combined diff with `--cc` semantics, which only shows files that differ from all parents AND requires the merge to be \"interesting\". But the stat output above lists many files — that suggests these files differ from both parents. Wait, is that right? For `.llm/runs/fix-readiness-fixture-app-identifier-collision--1898/*`: parent1 lacks these files (added by branch), parent2 has them. Result = parent2 version. Diff from parent1: added. Diff from parent2: none. So not in combined diff...\n\nUnless `git show --stat` on a merge defaults to `--first-parent` diff? Hmm, no. Let me think: `git show` for merges shows combined diff only when given `-m` off... Actually `git show <merge>` uses `--cc` which shows only \"interesting\" files. But the output above DID show files. Hmm, but maybe git's `--cc` shows files that differ from all parents... the listed files include BOTH run artifacts and the 2 product files.\n\nWait, actually I recall: `git show` on a merge commit shows the diff against first parent combined... no. Let me test empirically rather than reason from memory. The key facts I already have:\n- `git diff 96f074065^1 96f074065 --name-only` = exactly the #1898 run dir (16 files) + the 2 product paths. CLEAN — the merge brought in exactly the #1898 branch's changes, nothing else. No third-party file modified relative to the branch side.\n- `git diff 96f074065^2 96f074065 --name-only | wc -l` = 49 — what the merge brought in relative to the #1898 side = main's evolution (7d18ef104..159ede042) + the #1844 branch work. Expected.\n\nSo for the #1898 merge: result-vs-parent1 = exactly the #1898 delta. That's a clean union — no evil merge on the branch side. The combined-diff listing is likely because those run-artifact files and product files differ from both parents in some way... hmm, wait. Actually for prepare-readiness-fixture.ts: parent1 has the #1844-era version (with different content than #1898's), parent2 has #1898's version, result = #1898's version. Diff vs parent1: changed. Diff vs parent2: none. Still not combined.\n\nHmm, why did `git show --stat` list them then? OH — I bet `git show` on a merge with no explicit diff option shows the combined diff, and `--stat` in combined mode lists files that differ from ANY parent... Actually there's a subtlety: `--stat` with `--cc` shows files where the merge result differs from both parents. But maybe it fell back to showing the diff vs first parent. Honestly, the numbers in the stat (20 ++ for prepare-readiness-fixture.ts, 98 for the test) match the diff vs parent1 exactly (20 and 98... earlier the #1898 diff showed \"20 +++++++-\" and \"98\" vs a7206cd3d — wait earlier `git diff 96f074065^1 96f074065` listed the files; stat numbers: prepare-readiness-fixture.ts | 20 ++- and test | 98 ++-). And `git show --stat` for ad53835ee showed the test at 99. So the stat shown by `git show` for the merge matches diff-vs-first-parent. Fine — git's default for merge stat is vs first parent. Not evidence of conflict resolution.\n\nTo be rigorous about conflict resolution, the right check: does the merge result differ from BOTH parents for any file (a real combined diff)? Use `git show --cc --name-only 96f074065` or `git diff-tree --cc --name-only 96f074065`. Let me run that for both merges.\n\n**a7206cd3d** (merge origin/main): the stat vs first parent would show main's changes; the combined diff (`--cc`) is what matters. The output shown is `git show --stat` which for merges shows... the listed files include .agents/skills/aspire/SKILL.md, run dirs from OTHER branches (fix-workers-registry-compiler-parity, feat-sdk-client-s3--1349) — those are files added by main between the previous merge base and 159ede042. That's the vs-parent1 diff.\n\nNeed `--cc` for both merges. Also for fc0b26585 (the #1844 leaf merge, which showed combined-diff-looking output with .llm/tools/agentic changes — that merge predates the #1844 verdict, so it's inside the already-judged leaf; not my concern beyond noting).\n\n**81cee29ce**: only touches the #1844 run dir worklog.md (27 insertions) — run artifact only. Clean.\n\nNext steps:\n1. `git diff-tree --cc --name-only` for a7206cd3d and 96f074065 → true conflict resolutions.\n2. Full delta vs origin/main classification (G6).\n\nThen G3–G5: run the test suites. Let me kick off the focused test suites in the background while doing the merge forensics — actually let me first do the quick git checks, then start the tests (they're the long pole).\n\nActually, let me start the two focused test suites now in background (they may take minutes), and do git checks meanwhile. The tests:\n- `deno run --allow-read --allow-write --allow-run .llm/tools/run-deno-test.ts -- --allow-all packages/cli/e2e/tests/application/gates`\n- `deno run --allow-read --allow-write --allow-run .llm/tools/run-deno-test.ts -- --allow-all packages/cli/src/kernel/templates/aspire/helpers/tests`\n\nMemory note: no `rtk` in this env, so run deno directly (not `rtk proxy`). AGENTS.md says wrap in rtk proxy but memory says rtk isn't available in this eval env. Use plain commands with output to files in .llm/tmp or the run dir. The brief says \"Do not edit product code, commit anything but that verdict\" — writing gate logs to .llm/tmp is fine (temporary scratch belongs in .llm/tmp/).\n\nLet me create the task list too — this is a multi-step eval. Quick TaskCreate batch. Actually the reminder suggests it; I'll create 6 gate tasks... that's ceremony; but it helps tracking. I'll skip heavy task tracking and keep momentum — actually let me create a minimal set since this is a long multi-gate run. Hmm, the reminder is gentle. I'll create tasks for G2–G6 remaining work. Actually, let me just proceed — the structure is already dictated by the brief and I'm moving fast. I'll use tasks minimally: one for gates, one for verdict. Let me not over-engineer; proceed.\n\nBackground the two test suites now.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Parasail","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1ca789b-c4ca-4f53-b9ac-092eb2e122d5","uuid":"cd38c7d2-454a-4733-9ad7-9d97fa8f239b","timestamp":"2026-09-01T21:19:53.987Z","request_id":"gen-1788297485-bfxaQYWPOujG7rlzKuQy"}
