erty`, `ContainerLifetime`, `HealthStatus`, `HealthCheckResult`.\n\nBuilding a faithful DistributedApplicationBuilder stub: the generated code uses patterns like:\n- `builder.addParameter(name, { value, secret })` → promise-like with no further chaining\n- `builder.addPostgres(name, { port, password })` → chained `.withLifetime(...)`, `.withDataBindMount(...)`, `.withImage(...)`, `.withEnvironment(...)`, awaited\n- `builder.addContainer(name, image)` → chained `.withEndpoint(...)`, `.withBindMount(...)`, `.withContainerRuntimeArgs(...)`, `.withArgs(...)`, `.withEnvironment(...)`\n- `builder.addExecutable(name, command, workdir, args)` → `.withEndpoint(...)`\n- `await x.getEndpoint('tcp')` → EndpointReference with `.property(EndpointProperty.HostAndPort)`, `.host()`, `.port()`\n- `builder.addHealthCheck(key, async () => {...})`\n- `await resource.withHealthCheck(key)`\n- `builder.getConfiguration().getConfigValue('DcpPublisher:RandomizePorts')`\n- `resource.waitFor(...)`? Used in AspireResource type derivation: `Parameters<ReturnType<DistributedApplicationBuilder['addExecutable']>['waitFor']>[0]` — so the ExecutableResource type needs a `waitFor` method.\n\nThis is doable — I'll write the stub to match usage. The compat test's aspire.mts stub has EndpointReference etc. I'll extend it.\n\nLet me look at the rest of the compat test to see exactly how it wires deno.json (imports, compilerOptions) so my harness reuses the same conventions. Let me read lines 80-376 of the compat test.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Parasail","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1ca789b-c4ca-4f53-b9ac-092eb2e122d5","uuid":"8fc7cce4-