fine as const object typed via enum-ish. The generated code passes it to property(property: X) — as long as my stub's property() accepts that type, fine. I'll model:\n\n```ts\nexport enum EndpointProperty { Url = 'Url', HostAndPort = 'HostAndPort' }\nexport enum ContainerLifetime { Session = 'Session', Persistent = 'Persistent' }\n```\n\nBuilder interface — generated usage:\n- `builder.addParameter(name, { value, secret })` → awaited → ParameterResource (no methods used beyond being stored... actually db_<n> parameter used as value only).\n- `builder.addPostgres(name, { port, password })` → PostgresServerResourceBuilder with chain: withLifetime(x), withDataBindMount(p), withImage(i), withImageTag(t), withEnvironment(k, v) — chained AND awaited: `const db_0_server = await builder.addPostgres(...).withLifetime(...)...;` So the chain methods must return the same type (promise-like with the methods) — the real SDK returns a \"ResourcePromise\" that has the chain methods AND is awaitable to the resource. I'll model a single type that is both: interface with methods + PromiseLike<itself>? Simplest faithful: \n\n```ts\nexport interface PostgresServerResource {\n  withLifetime(lifetime: ContainerLifetime): PostgresServerResourceBuilder;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, to keep this manageable, model a generic \"ResourcePromise<T>\" pattern:\n\n```ts\ntype Thenable<T> = PromiseLike<T>;\n```\n\nThe generated code awaits the whole chain, and stores result as resource used later for `.getEndpoint`, `.withHealthCheck`, `.addDatabase`. So the awaited type must have getEndpoint/withHealthCheck. And the pre-await chain must have withLifetime/withDataBindMount/withImage/withEnvironment. And after await, `db_0_server.getEndpoint('tcp')` is awaited → EndpointReference... wait in the postgres block: `const endpoint = await db_0_server.getEndpoint('tcp');` — inside the addHealthCheck callback. And `await db_0_server.withHealthCheck('postgres_listener')`.\n\nSo define:\n\n```ts\nexport interface HealthCheckCallback { (): HealthCheckResult | Promise<HealthCheckResult>; }\n\nexport interface ResourceBuilderBase {\n  withEnvironment(key: string, value: string): ResourcePromise<Resource>;\n  getEndpoint(scheme: string): EndpointReferencePromise;\n  withHealthCheck(key: string): Promise<void>;\n  waitFor(resource: Resource): Promise<void>;  // for AspireResource derivation\n}\n```\n\nHmm, wait. The AspireResource derivation: `Parameters<ReturnType<DistributedApplicationBuilder['addExecutable']>['waitFor']>[0]`. If addExecutable returns ExecutableResource with waitFor(resource: X): Promise<void>, then AspireResource = X. I'll define X as an interface `AspireResourceModel { withHealthCheck(key: string): Promise<void>; getEndpoint(scheme: string): EndpointReferencePromise; }` and have resources extend it. This keeps the derivation honest.\n\nLet me design the stub carefully but compactly:\n\n```ts\nexport enum HealthStatus { Unhealthy = 'Unhealthy', Degraded = 'Degraded', Healthy = 'Healthy' }\nexport interface HealthCheckResult { status?: HealthStatus; description?: string | null; data?: Record<string, string>; }\nexport enum EndpointProperty { Url = 'Url', HostAndPort = 'HostAndPort' }\nexport enum ContainerLifetime { Session = 'Session', Persistent = 'Persistent' }\nexport interface EndpointReference { port(): Promise<number>; host(): Promise<string>; property(property: EndpointProperty): string; }\nexport interface EndpointReferencePromise extends PromiseLike<EndpointReference> { port(): Promise<number>; host(): Promise<string>; }\n\nexport interface ExecutableResource {\n  withEndpoint(options: Record<string, unknown>): ExecutableResourcePromise;\n  withEnvironment(key: string, value: string): ExecutableResourcePromise;\n  getEndpoint(scheme: string): EndpointReferencePromise;\n  withHealthCheck(key: string): Promise<void>;\n  waitFor(resource: ExecutableResource): PromiseLike<void>;\n}\nexport interface ExecutableResourcePromise extends PromiseLike<ExecutableResource>, ExecutableResource {}\n```\n\nHmm — `interface X extends PromiseLike<ExecutableResource>, ExecutableResource {}` — that's the ResourcePromise pattern. OK.\n\nContainerResource: withEndpoint, withBindMount(source, target), withContainerRuntimeArgs(args: string[]), withArgs(args: string[]), withEnvironment, getEndpoint, withHealthCheck, waitFor.\nPostgresServerResource: withLifetime(l: ContainerLifetime), withDataBindMount(path), withImage(image), withImageTag(tag), withEnvironment, getEndpoint, withHealthCheck, addDatabase(name): PromiseLike<...resource>, waitFor.\nParameterResource: {} (value only).\n\nDistributedApplicationBuilder:\n- addExecutable(name, command, workdir, args): ExecutableResourcePromise\n- addContainer(name, image): ContainerResourcePromise\n- addPostgres(name, options): PostgresServerResourcePromise\n- addParameter(name, options): PromiseLike<ParameterResource>  — generated: `await builder.addParameter(...)` no chain.\n- addConnectionString(name): PromiseLike<...>\n- addHealthCheck(key: string, check: () => HealthCheckResult | Promise<HealthCheckResult>): void\n- getConfiguration(): { getConfigValue(key: string): PromiseLike<string | undefined> }\n\nHmm wait — in the generated postgres block: `const db_0_server = await builder.addPostgres(...)` chained — with `await` at the front of the whole chain. And inside addHealthCheck callback: `const endpoint = await db_0_server.getEndpoint('tcp');` — so the RESOLVED type (PostgresServerResource) must have getEndpoint returning EndpointReferencePromise (awaitable). And `await db_0_server.withHealthCheck('postgres_listener')` → Promise<void>. Fine.\n\nFor the cache executable arm: `const cache_0 = await builder.addExecutable(...).withEndpoint(...)` — withEndpoint returns ExecutableResourcePromise (still chainable/awaitable) → awaiting gives ExecutableResource, which needs getEndpoint + withHealthCheck. ✓.\n\nContainer arm: `const cache_0 = await builder.addContainer(...).withEndpoint(...).withContainerRuntimeArgs([...]).withArgs([...]).withEnvironment(...)` → ContainerResource with getEndpoint/withHealthCheck. ✓.\n\nAlso generated code: `cacheWiring.set(name, { resource: cache_0, reference: cache_0_tcpEndpoint, env: {...}, local: false })` — CacheWiring type from compat: let me check its shape (line 575): `{ resource: unknown-ish; reference: unknown; env: Record<string,string>; local: boolean }` — likely `resource: ExecutableResource | ContainerResource`? It's in the compat template; it may type resource as `unknown` or a minimal shape. Since the template's CacheWiring is defined in the compat template itself, whatever it says governs. Let me check lines 575-596 quickly. Also `cacheEndpoints.set(name, cache_0_tcpEndpoint)` — Map<string, EndpointReference>. cache_0_tcpEndpoint = await cache_0.getEndpoint('tcp') → EndpointReference (awaited). ✓. And `cache_0_hostPort = cache_0_tcpEndpoint.property(EndpointProperty.HostAndPort)` returns string. ✓.\n\nAlso `databases.set(name, db_0)` where db_0 = db_0_server (PostgresServerResource) or db_0_server.addDatabase(...) — Map<string, AspireResource> where AspireResource = Parameters<ReturnType<DAB['addExecutable']>['waitFor']>[0]. If addExecutable's waitFor takes `ExecutableResource`, then AspireResource = ExecutableResource. Is PostgresServerResource assignable to ExecutableResource? It has all the methods? PostgresServerResource needs: withEnvironment? (yes generated uses withEnvironment on mssql only), getEndpoint, withHealthCheck, waitFor — my PostgresServerResource must structurally include waitFor(resource: ExecutableResource) to be assignable to Map<string, AspireResource=ExecutableResource>. Method parameter bivariance in TS methods makes it OK-ish. To be safe, define a common base interface with waitFor(resource: ExecutableResource) and have all resources extend it. Let me check CacheWiring's definition first.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequenc