ise<void> return type would break chaining. In the fixture test the stub's withEnvironment returns Promise<void> and the generated code does `await app_0.withEnvironment(...)` — presumably each statement awaited separately. OK — whatever; the existing test passes with that stub, and my combined stub only needs to be compatible with BOTH modules' usage. My base's withEnvironment returns chainable — that's a superset of Promise<void>-compatibility? register-apps generated code: `await app_0.withEnvironment(...)` — awaiting a chainable object is fine.\n\nPotential conflict: register-apps' stub had `withHttpHealthCheck(options: unknown): Promise<void>` — generated code might chain `.withHttpHealthCheck(...)` mid-chain (e.g., `await app.withHttpHealthCheck({...}).withBrowserLogs()`)? If the generated code awaits at the end of a chain, intermediate methods must return chainable. My base returns chainable — safe.\n\nLet me now also confirm what register-apps generated code actually calls, by generating it in the script and — no, the type-check will tell me. Iterate if needed.\n\nEnv for deno check: the compat template imports 'zod' — deno.json imports map with zod specifier. Also node: builtins — fine.\n\n`deno check --config <path> --no-lock <file>`: the config path must be absolute or relative to cwd; use cwd=root. I'll run Deno.Command with cwd: root.\n\nAlso — IMPORTANT — the injected infra references `shouldUseContainerCache` etc. imported from './_aspire-compat.mts' via ASPIRE_COMPAT_IMPORT = './_aspire-compat.mts'. ✓ And the compat template imports `HealthStatus` (VALUE) and `HealthCheckResult` (type) from '../.aspire/modules/aspire.mts' → my stub must export the HealthStatus enum as a value. ✓\n\nregister-apps imports `OtlpProtocol` (value) + DistributedApplicationBuilder (type). ✓\n\nThe compat template also imports zod and uses `z.looseObject` etc. — needs the workspace's zod version. `import.meta.resolve('zod')` from within the CLI package gives the repo's zod. The compat test did exactly that and passed.\n\nAlright — also one more composition angle for G3: `prepareReadinessFixture` injects APPS first (register-apps.mts), then health checks (register-infrastructure.mts) — and CRITICALLY reads register-infrastructure BEFORE writeControllerFixture, and the `injectListenerFaultHealthChecks` THROWS if the keys already exist (fail-closed on double-registration). Composition on real output = the order in prepareReadinessFixture. My script mirrors it.\n\nExtra composition risk worth testing: what if the REAL project's app is named such that its binding is literally `readiness_fixture_app_0`? Contrived; skip. But a REAL risk: a project with an app named `readiness-fixture-...`? The fixture names are `readiness-dead-port` and `listener-fault-controller`; if the real project had an app with one of those names, `appRegistrations(injected, name).length > 0` throws (fail-closed). Good — already guarded.\n\nAnother REAL interaction: the health injection uses `healthAttachments(source, 'garnet_resp')` — matching ALL `withHealthCheck('garnet_resp')` lines. If a real project has TWO Garnet caches (e.g., garnet + redis), there'd be more attachments — fine, injects after each. And the `postgres_listener` key is derived from the db NAME (`${name}_listener`): the injection hardcodes POSTGRES_REAL_HEALTH_KEY = 'postgres_listener' — matching only a database literally named 'postgres'. If the project's db were named differently, injectListenerFaultHealthChecks would throw (fail-closed) — acceptable design; the E2E fixture uses 'postgres'. Not a finding.\n\nLet me also verify the D-101 e2e actually uses Garnet Mode 'Auto' + Postgres Container — check `listener-readiness-gates.ts` buildCacheBlock usage... The existing test used `buildCacheBlock('garnet')` with Mode 'Auto'. Let me check what the gates generate — grep for buildCacheBlock / Auto in the gates dir to confirm the E2E config shape. Actually `listenerInfrastructureReference()` in prepare-readiness-fixture.ts uses Garnet Mode 'Container' as the REFERENCE (to assert the generator emits attachments at all) — and the real fixture project is generated by the scaffold with the D-101 config. The first test (\"accepts the E2E two-cache Auto generator output\") uses redis Container + garnet Auto with SQLITE. My proof uses Postgres Container + garnet Auto (the strongest: both keys, 2 Auto arms). I'll ALSO include the two-cache variant (redis + garnet Auto) since that's the actual E2E shape from the test. Let me do both configs in one script: config A = postgres container + garnet auto (D-101 postgres), config B = sqlite + redis container + garnet auto (E2E sqlite shape).\n\nFor config B, `injectListenerFaultHealthChecks(source, DATABASE.SQLITE)` → garnet-only (includePostgres=false). Assertions: TEST_ONLY_GARNET === 2 (both arms), no TEST_ONLY_POSTGRES.\n\nNow — write the script. One more import path check: `packages/cli/e2e/src/domain/extension-axes.ts` — from `.llm/tmp/`: '../../packages/cli/e2e/src/domain/extension-axes.ts'. And prepare-readiness-fixture imports itself use deep relative paths into src/kernel — those resolve relative to their own file, fine.\n\ndeno check of the e2e gates test imports — my script imports from packages/cli/e2e/src/... which may rely on workspace imports (@std/assert etc.)? prepare-readiness-fixture.ts imports only local files + template registry (which hydrates embedded assets — `DEFAULT_TEMPLATE_REGISTRY.hydrate()` needed? injectReadinessFixtureApps calls generateRegisterApps directly (no registry hydration needed since generators are direct function calls). injectListenerFaultHealthChecks calls generateRegisterInfrastructure directly. renderTemplateAssetSync — reads embedded.generated.ts asset — should work in-repo. The existing tests import these same modules and pass, and my script runs with the workspace deno.json auto-discovered? If I run `deno run --allow-all .llm/tmp/g3-composition-proof.mts` from the worktree root, deno.json at root applies (workspace imports like @netscript/aspire/types used by the generators — types only, fine at runtime; type-check of my script isn't needed since I run without --check... but `deno run` type-checks by default! With workspace config it should resolve @std/assert via jsr from the lock. The existing tests run under `deno test --allow-all` with the root config. My script imports the same modules → same deps. generate-register-apps imports types from '@netscript/aspire/types' — type-only, resolvable in workspace. OK.\n\nTo avoid my script's own type errors blocking (`deno run` type-checks), keep it type-tight. I'll write carefully, and run with `--no-check`? Better: let it type-check — stronger. But risk of trivial TS friction. I'll use pragmatic types (strings, no fancy generics) and `// deno-lint-ignore` isn't needed for run. Let me write it.\n\nFor the temp workspace type-check, run: `deno check --config <root>/deno.json --no-lock <helpers>/register-apps.mts` and `<helpers>/register-infrastructure.mts` (both, explicitly).\n\nAlso assert the injected register-infrastructure has EXACTLY one test-only registration per Auto arm:\n- Split arms: find index of the cache arm block: lines between `  if (shouldUseContainerCache()) {` (first occurrence after `// cache 0 (Auto`) and its closing. I'll implement: find lines containing `withHealthCheck(\"garnet_resp\")` → realAttachmentLines (2). elseLine = first line after realAttachmentLines[0] with trim === '} else {'. Arm1 = lines[realAttachmentLines[0] .. elseLine], Arm2 = lines[elseLine .. realAttachmentLines[1]]. Count `builder.addHealthCheck('test_only_garnet_resp'` in each === 1 each. (Mirrors assertAutoBranchInjectionPlacement but stricter: exactly one per arm.)\n\nWrite the script now. Also write aspire.mts stub. For `deno check` to resolve '../.aspire/modules/aspire.mts' from .helpers — path: root/aspire/.helpers/x.mts → '../.aspire/modules/aspire.mts' = root/aspire/.aspire/modules/aspire.mts. ✓\n\nregister-apps.mts imports './register-infrastructure.mts' (type InfrastructureContext) — same dir ✓.\n\nThe compat template uses `import { z } from 'zod'` → mapped. Uses node: builtins → allowed by default in check? `deno check` handles node: specifiers natively. The compat test proved it.\n\nFile placement: `.llm/tmp/g3-composition-proof.mts`. Output: console log lines with PASS/FAIL + a final summary; I'll capture to a log.\n\nLet me write it.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Parasail","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1ca789b-c4ca-4f53-b9ac-092eb2e122d5","uuid":"b3fe289b-f261-4589-a681-7c4fd0bcd4dd","timestamp":"2026-09-01T21:39:34.079Z","request_id":"gen-1788298527-m6dQFNB1LMrhmPfJbjru"}
