{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788299294-demktCSTUjakBEypszu6","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Writing works fine. So why did `deno check` see no exports? \n\nOH — I see it now. The check command: `deno check --config ${root}/deno.json --no-lock ${helpers}/register-infrastructure.mts` with `cwd: root`. The config at root/deno.json has `imports: { zod: 'npm:zod@4.4.3' }`. The aspire.mts file... \n\nHmm wait, maybe the problem is different: TS2305 for EVERY import from aspire.mts including types — as if the module resolved to an EMPTY file. Is it possible `Deno.writeTextFile(modules/aspire.mts, ...)` wrote to the wrong path? I wrote `${modules}/aspire.mts` where modules = `${root}/aspire/.aspire/modules` — mkdir recursive created it. The error path IS that exact path.\n\nWait — could `deno check` have checked a DIFFERENT aspire.mts? No, paths match.\n\nCould the issue be that `deno check` with `--no-lock` and an `imports` map... the TS2305 errors were only 5 (in the first check) and the output was truncated at 3000 chars for the second. The common thread: EVERY named import from aspire.mts fails. If the file had a top-level syntax error, deno check reports the syntax error instead. If the file exports those names... \n\nHmm, unless the write to aspire.mts silently failed because the modules dir wasn't created — mkdir recursive created both. If the file didn't exist, the error would be \"Cannot find module\" (TS2307), not TS2305. So the file EXISTS but exports nothing... \n\nOH WAIT. I know what it might be. My stub function returns a template literal that starts with `\\n` and the enums use `export enum` — fine. But hold on, in the ACTUAL run, did I write the stub BEFORE running check? Yes, before the loop.\n\nHmm, let me look at my stub string again... `export enum HealthStatus {` — with `Unhealthy = 'Unhealthy',` — single quotes inside a template literal — fine.\n\nActually — maybe the problem