ition — 30929 ms FAILED. That's razor-thin: the gate is borderline-timing. ECONNREFUSED \"after 1 ms\" — the controller listener was NOT listening the whole 30 s (1 ms refusal = no listener). Hmm, that's not slowness of the transition — the poll kept getting ECONNREFUSED for the full 30 s window. So the controller was NOT listening on 18999 at all during the window — then at the #1898 leaf it apparently started listening by ~30 s (30558 ms total gate time including fault injection + recovery?).\n\nWait — how does the controller bind 18999? The controller app is a Deno task started by Aspire as an app resource. It listens on 18998/18999 (postgres/garnet controller listeners) controlled by the state file (postgresOpen/garnetOpen). The health check pings localhost:18999 (the controller's Garnet proxy listener). If the controller app isn't running/listening, ECONNREFUSED.\n\nKey question: did the controller app start? Possible causes:\n1. The app registration injection failed silently → app not registered → nothing listening. But then the verifier's own controller-health expectations would fail elsewhere... Actually the verifier polls the apphost's health report for `test_only_garnet_resp` — the injected health check — which is registered (the report EXISTS — \"garnet healthReports.test_only_garnet_resp ... last=Unhealthy\"). So the injected health check IS registered and evaluating (the #1844 injection worked — the check exists in the report and reports Unhealthy with RESP ECONNREFUSED details). Only the CONTROLLER listener is missing.\n2. The controller app crashed at startup on the runner (e.g., permission, missing file, listen conflict). The fixture controller writes to LISTENER_FAULT_STATE_FILE; the app runs `deno run --allow-net --allow-read --allow-write main.ts` with Workdir LISTENER_FAULT_CONTROLLER_DIR.\n\nHmm — but is the controller's binding of 18999 conditional on the fault state? The controller's `start` task runs main.ts which reads the state file and opens/closes listeners. Initial state `{revision: 0, postgresOpen: true, garnetOpen: true}` → both open. So it should listen on both ports from the start.\n\nOn the hosted runner BOTH tiers failed with (presumably) the same signature. Let me check the postgres tier log for its failure signature — if postgres tier failed at the same 18999 ECONNREFUSED (rather than its own 18998 postgres check or the #1844 wall), that suggests a common cause: the controller app not starting on the runner.\n\nIMPORTANT cross-check: the worklog table says at 438ed4f60 ALONE the sqlite tier PASSED listener-unreachable in 30558 ms. The #1898 branch ALSO ran its hosted proof? Or was that a local run? \"PASS 30558 ms\" — presumably from the #1898 IMPL-EVAL hosted run. So the same gate passed at 438ed4f60 on sqlite. At the combined head it fails. What changed between 438ed4f60 and 81cee29ce on this path?\n- #1844's changes (verify-listener-readiness.ts, listener-readiness-gates.ts, generate-register-infrastructure.ts, _aspire-compat template, embedded barrels) — the RESP probe rewrite and readiness verifier rewrite.\n- main's evolution (many commits).\n- The #1898 files themselves are byte-identical (G1 verified).\n\nThe failing piece is the interaction: the injected `createRespPingCheck({host:'localhost', port:18999})` — #1844 REWROTE createRespPingCheck in the compat template (the RESP probe rewrite!). At 438ed4f60, the test-only check used the OLD probe; at the combined head it uses the NEW #1844 probe. If the new probe has a stricter behavior (e.g., different connect semantics on the runner — IPv6/IPv4 resolution of 'localhost'?!), ECONNREFUSED could result.\n\n**The localhost hypothesis**: the new RESP probe may connect to `localhost:18999`. On the runner, if the controller binds `127.0.0.1` explicitly and the probe resolves `localhost` to `::1` (IPv6 first), the connection to ::1:18999 would be refused while 127.0.0.1:18999 is listening. OR the controller binds 0.0.0.0 and ::1 works. This